  "notes": "Measured off the public-domain Spangdahlem photo of N821SY (left side, 2016-10-20), fitted to shapes/B738.json: the stripe's top at y about -0.42 and its bottom -0.54 along the cabin, lower at the nose (it meets the radome near its foot) and rising to the tail cone; the light-blue field from x 0.04 to 0.27; the title from x 0.209 to 0.429, baseline 0.27, caps 0.37 (the photo's baseline reads 0.29 at the left end and 0.25 at the right: averaged). The fin's navy cap from 0.884 of the height at the leading edge to 0.83 at the trailing edge; the sun centered at fin (0.55, 0.39), its rays' tips 0.8 of the fin's height across, the S 0.44. The forward sun's S runs from the crown to the stripe (y 0.70 to -0.38 at x 0.174) with rays from x 0.09 to 0.24; its navy is drawn only as the logo, and the darker blue under the cockpit windows and over the lower nose (part of its rays) is left base blue. Colors by eye off the 2019 photo in sun. Not drawn: the registration and 'BOEING 737-800' on the aft fuselage. The 737-700s (2008-2023) wore it too; none is left to tag, so no 737-700 paint is drawn. N805SY (2026) still wears it.",
  "history": "Sun Country's scheme from its 2001-2002 relaunch (Wikipedia: 2001-2016). Simplified in December 2016 (blue-2016: no forward sun, 'SUN COUNTRY AIRLINES' titles), then replaced by the 2018 livery (standard). A few 737-800s still wear it in 2026; they leave it as they're repainted."
